{"path":"SUMMARY.md","content":"# Summary\n\n## Stage 1 — money.py\nImplemented `parse_amount` following the ordered steps: type check, strip, negative forms (parentheses reject an inner sign, leading \"+\" is always rejected), optional \"$\" only after the sign, then two regex shapes for the magnitude (with/without comma grouping). `format_amount` uses `divmod` and `\"{:,}\"` with the minus placed before the \"$\". The edge case most likely to be got wrong: \"0.5\" must right-pad to 50 cents (not 5), and \"-$5\"/\"($5)\" are valid while \"$-5\" is not because the \"$\" is consumed before the sign check on the remainder.\n\n## Stage 2 — parsing.py\n`parse_line` enforces exactly three pipe-separated fields, a date that passes BOTH a `YYYY-MM-DD` shape regex and a real-calendar check, a non-empty description, and converts the single `ValueError` from `money.parse_amount` into a `LedgerError`. `parse_lines` returns a 2-tuple of (txns, errors) with 1-based line numbers that count skipped comment/blank lines. The edge case most likely to be got wrong: `datetime.date.fromisoformat` alone accepts \"20240105\", so the shape regex is not redundant and both checks are required.\n\n## Stage 3 — rules.py\n`load_rules` skips only \";\" comments (a line starting with \"#\" is a real rule, per spec/00b), requires exactly three \"=\" parts, and lets `int()` raise naturally for bad priorities. `categorize` does case-insensitive substring matching of the pattern against the desc, picks the highest priority, and uses strict `>` so ties keep the earlier rule in file order. The edge case most likely to be got wrong: treating \"#\" as a rules-file comment character — it is not; only \";\" is.\n\n## Stage 4 — report.py\n`summarize` groups txns by category into 3-tuples sorted by total ascending with ties broken by plain case-sensitive category-name comparison. `render_report` renders in the given order (never re-sorting) with exact 15/12/6 ljust/rjust columns, no truncation of long names, no trailing newline, and still four lines for empty input. The edge case most likely to be got wrong: the tie-break is case-sensitive plain `str` comparison (\"Zebra\" before \"apple\"), not a casefolded sort.\n\n## Stage 5 — tags (extension)\nChanged `parse_line` to accept three OR four fields; the fourth is split on \",\", each element stripped and lower-cased, empty elements dropped so trailing commas are harmless. Changed `categorize` so a pattern starting with \"#\" matches by exact lower-cased equality against one of the txn's tags — never substring, and it does not match the description at all. The edge case most likely to be got wrong: \"#daily\" must NOT match the tag \"dailyish\" or a desc containing \"daily\"; tag matching is full-string equality only.\n\n## Stage 6 — cli.py\n`main(argv)` uses argparse with a positional \"report\" command, required `--ledger`/`--rules`, and an optional `--strict`. It reads both files first (OSError -> \"cannot read <path>\" on stderr, exit 2), then loads rules (ValueError -> \"rules error: <message>\", exit 2), then parses the ledger; with `--strict` and errors it prints \"line <n>: <message>\" per error to stderr and returns 1 with nothing on stdout, otherwise it prints the report to stdout and, if there were non-strict errors, a \"warning: <k> malformed line(s) skipped\" line to stderr while still returning 0. The edge case most likely to be got wrong: the strict path must print NOTHING to stdout (no report), and a clean successful run writes nothing at all to stderr.\n"}